---
import SwissLayout from '../layouts/SwissLayout.astro';
import SwissHeader from '../components/SwissHeader.astro';
import SwissFooter from '../components/SwissFooter.astro';
---

<SwissLayout
  title="Laurent Nyssen — Colophon"
  description="How this site is made: one typeface, no third-party requests, and a grid you can look at."
>
  <SwissHeader />

  <main>
    <section class="swiss-shell pt-[40px] md:pt-[72px]">
      <h1 class="swiss-display">Colophon<span class="text-[var(--accent)]">.</span></h1>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <p class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          Why this page
        </p>

        <p class="swiss-lede col-span-6 md:col-span-8">
          A book says on its last page how it was made. A site can do the same.
          Everything below is a decision, not a default.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <p class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          Type
        </p>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-8 md:text-[17px]">
          <p>
            Set entirely in <strong class="font-medium text-[var(--ink)]">Switzer</strong>,
            drawn by the Indian Type Foundry and released under their free font
            licence. Five weights are used, from light to bold. One family for
            the whole site, headlines and captions alike: the hierarchy comes
            from size, weight and space, which is the harder way and the one
            that holds together.
          </p>
          <p>
            The files are served from this domain rather than from a font
            network, so no third party learns your address when a page loads.
            The two weights visible on arrival are preloaded, which is why the
            text does not change width a moment after it appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            Vertical spacing is set with <code>text-box: trim-both cap
            alphabetic</code>, a recent CSS property that measures from the top
            of the capitals to the baseline instead of from the invisible box
            around them. It is the reason a label in the left column sits
            exactly on the line of the text beside it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <p class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          Grid
        </p>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-8 md:text-[17px]">
          <p>
            Twelve columns on a 1200&nbsp;pixel measure, six on a phone. Every
            section breathes by the same single value, above the rule and below
            the last line.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can look at it: the button in the header shows the columns, or
            press <strong class="font-medium text-[var(--ink)]">G</strong>. The
            arrow keys walk the index, Enter opens a project, Escape closes it,
            and the left and right arrows move through its visuals.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <p class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          Build
        </p>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-8 md:text-[17px]">
          <p>
            Built with Astro and hosted on Vercel. The pages are plain HTML,
            generated once at build time; JavaScript runs only where something
            actually moves, which is the cursor, the theme switch and the
            reveal on scroll. Everything else, including the accordion that
            opens each project, is done by the browser on its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            The projects live as text files in a private repository. Adding one
            writes a commit, and the commit rebuilds the site. Images are
            converted to WebP at build time and served at the size they are
            displayed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dark and light follow your system until you use the switch, after
            which your choice is remembered on your device alone. The paper
            grain is a single tiling image, and it sits above everything so the
            texture never breaks at the edge of a bar.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <p class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          What it costs you
        </p>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-8 md:text-[17px]">
          <p>
            Nothing is loaded from another domain. No cookies, no advertising,
            no profile. Page views are counted without identifying anyone, and
            the <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a> says exactly what that
            means in the language the law asks for.
          </p>
          <p>
            The site prints. If you send it to paper or to PDF, the bars and
            the grain drop away, the palette turns black on white, the projects
            unfold, and every link shows the address it points to.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SwissFooter />
</SwissLayout>
